## waypoint_router.py internals

- **`_scan_waypoints(stage)`** — finds all `/World/nav_waypoint_*` prims, extracts world positions.
- **`_build_caches(stage)`** — scans all waypoints; separates entrance waypoints (auto-detected by `_ENTRANCE_PREFIX`), filters out branch + entrance names, sorts the remaining into the corridor ring by angular position; also builds `_all_waypoints` lookup dict and `_entrances` list. Cached until `invalidate()`.
- **`_navmesh_distance(a, b)`** — actual walkable distance via `navmesh_path_length_cm()` (lightweight query, skips full `calculate_path_points` pipeline). Returns `float('inf')` on failure.
- **`_nearest_ring_by_navmesh(ring, pos)`** — finds the ring waypoint with shortest NavMesh walk from `pos`. Evaluates top `_START_CANDIDATES` XZ-nearest, queries NavMesh for top `_MAX_VALIDATION_QUERIES`. No destination bias. Returns `(ring_index, navmesh_distance_cm)`. Used for ring joining from entrance and for entrance-vs-ring detection (both sides).
- **`_is_behind_ring_candidate(anchor_pos, cand_idx, other_idx, ring)`** — forward-bias test. Returns True when the anchor has already passed `ring[cand_idx]` along the shorter-arc toward `ring[other_idx]` (dot of `(anchor − cand)` against the first forward ring step `(next − cand)` > 0). Prevents the "cost function picks a just-behind waypoint because its XZ is tiny" failure that showed up on pause/recalc of already-in-progress routes (bird-eye `poi_nav` especially).
- **`_is_past_entrance(anchor_pos, ent_pos, ring_join_pos)`** — entrance forward-bias test. Returns True when the anchor has crossed the entrance into the ring area (anchor's offset from entrance projects positively onto the inward `entrance → ring_join` vector). Without it, a mid-route recalc where the player has just stepped past the door can still see the entrance as the NavMesh-nearest approach point and route them 180° back out.
- **`_pick_endpoint(ring, entrances, pos, other_idx, *, label="Start")`** — direction-agnostic entrance-vs-ring decision. Nearest entrance closer → check `_is_past_entrance` first; if anchor is already past it, skip and fall through to ring. Ring closer → use `_best_ring_index`. Called twice by `plan_route_to_position` (`label="Start"` then `label="End"`); called once by `plan_route` (seat path, `label="Start"`). `label` is purely for log clarity. Returns `(ring_idx, entrance_pos_or_None)`.
- **`_best_ring_index(ring, anchor_pos, other_idx)`** — cost-optimised ring node near `anchor_pos`. Scores top `_START_CANDIDATES` (5) nearest ring waypoints as `XZ(anchor → candidate) + ring_travel_distance(candidate → other_idx)`, then filters out behind-anchor candidates via `_is_behind_ring_candidate` (keeping all as fallback when none are forward). Validates remaining with a NavMesh-walk ÷ XZ-span **wall-check ratio** (`_WALL_CHECK_RATIO`). Real indoor detours (stairs, floor change) can exceed a tight threshold without crossing geometry — if the ratio is set too low, candidates such as `nav_waypoint_UA` are skipped spuriously and `plan_route_to_position` can return **degenerate via lists**, which breaks or weakens `RouteComposer` for some POI targets (more visible when **shortcuts are off**). Direction-agnostic — `_ring_travel_distance` returns the shorter arc, so the same cost function works for both start (`anchor=player`, `other_idx=end_idx`) and end (`anchor=target`, `other_idx=start_idx`).
- **`_ring_travel_distance(ring, from_idx, to_idx)`** — sum of XZ distances between consecutive waypoints along the shorter ring traversal.
- **`_exit_index_for_section(ring, section, all_wps)`** — for upper sections, finds the nearest ring waypoint to the staircase access point (dynamically by position); for ring sections, returns the index of `nav_waypoint_{section}`.
- **`_ring_path(ring_len, from_idx, to_idx)`** — returns the shorter traversal (CW or CCW) around the ring.
- **`plan_route_to_position(player_pos, target_pos)`** — generic entry point for non-seat POIs (restrooms, quiet zones, exits). **Bidirectional**: picks both endpoints with `_pick_endpoint`, so the route can enter AND exit the corridor through the best entrance. Forward routes (outdoor → POI) and reverse routes (POI → outdoor) are produced by the same code path. Edge cases handled inline: `entrance_in == entrance_out` collapses to a single via-point; `start_idx == end_idx` with no entrances skips the redundant ring node when both endpoints are within `SKIP_THRESHOLD_CM`. Used by `_on_calc` for `poi_nav` / `quiet_zone_nav` and by `compute_routes_to_exits` for waypoint-based distance estimation.
- **`prune_passed_via_points(player_pos, via_points, end_pos=None, *, reached_threshold_cm=VIA_POINT_REACHED_CM)`** — pure geometric helper shared by every surface that consumes corridor via-points. Sequential dot-product forward-bias test from the front of the via-list (drops `wp[i]` when the player is "ahead" of it along `wp[i] → wp[i+1]`, or `wp[-1] → end_pos` for the last waypoint), plus a head-of-list reached-radius drop after pruning. Called by `RouteInstance._compute_path` (engine) **and** by `RouteComposer._plan_intra_island_via` (composer's auto-corridor branch). Without the shared call, the bird-eye preview re-introduced ring waypoints the engine had pruned and rendered them as a spurious "extra arm" on the dashed overlay (most visible when the player was already inside the corridor and asked for directions to an outdoor map-marker spawn). The two surfaces must always go through this helper — divergence here is invisible until a user reports geometry drift between preview and committed route.
- **`plan_route(player_pos, seat_pos, section, *, floor_hint_y=None, branch_join_pos=None)`** — seat-specific entry point; calls `_pick_endpoint(label="Start")`, builds ring path (unless mid-branch rejoin skips it — see below), optimises exit ±1 for upper sections, appends branch chain. Intentionally **start-side-only** (no end-side `_pick_endpoint`): the seat is the canonical exit and an end-side entrance has no meaning. Before returning, applies the legacy single-sided ring-entry shortcut: if the seat is XZ-closer than the first via-point, returns `[]` (direct NavMesh routing). Returns ordered via-point positions.

**Post–vertical-move kwargs (elevators / seat resume):** used only for sections in `UPPER_SECTION_BRANCH`. **`floor_hint_y`** trims branch names whose prim **Y** is below `floor_hint_y - _DEFAULT_BRANCH_FLOOR_TOL_CM` (450 cm). **`branch_join_pos`** (player world position at lift exit or resume) selects a **suffix** of the trimmed chain via `_suffix_upper_branch_from_join_pos`: nearest chain waypoint in **3D** among staircase + intermediates only; if within **`_JOIN_AT_SECTION_ANCHOR_MAX_CM`** (700 cm) of the **section anchor** (last chain prim), suffix is **anchor-only**. When the first kept branch prim ≠ staircase head, the **main ring** segment is omitted (entrance prepend unchanged). Wired from `route_composer` post-hop leg and `route_engine/compute.py` seat resume when `corridor_section` is set — same `plan_route` + section as non-shortcut `seat_nav` (see `shortcuts.mdc`).

## `navmesh_route_service` via-point handling

- **`RouteConfig.via_points`** — list of `(x, y, z)` tuples representing intermediate waypoints.
- **`set_route_for_id(..., via_points=None)`** — `None` = inherit from existing route (preserves waypoints during auto-move recalc); `[]` = explicitly clear.
- **Multi-segment leg construction** — handled inside `RouteComposer.compose(via_points=…)` (see `route-composer.mdc`). The composer builds one `_navmesh_leg` per via-point pair, concatenates the polylines, and deduplicates junction points. The route engine (`route_engine/compute.py`: `RouteInstance._compute_path`) just passes the pruned via-list through to the composer alongside cost dicts and `enable_corridor_routing` (driven by `RouteConfig.use_composer`).
- **`_prune_passed_via_points(player_pos, via_points, end_pos=None)`** — thin per-route logging wrapper around `waypoint_router.prune_passed_via_points` (above). Owns only the `[NAVMESH_ROUTE:<id>] Pruned …` line — all algorithmic logic lives in the shared helper so the engine and the composer's auto-corridor branch cannot drift.
- **Measurement** — drawn routes go through `compose_with_measure()` (in `route_composer/measure.py`); the legacy single-segment `compute_route_measure` is no longer used by the engine and survives only for the bulk exits widget.

## Constants

| Constant | File | Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `SKIP_THRESHOLD_CM` | `waypoint_router.py` | 200 | Skip waypoint if player is already this close |
| `VIA_POINT_REACHED_CM` | `waypoint_router.py` | 300 | Default head-of-list reached-radius for `prune_passed_via_points` — also the engine's runtime "you've reached this waypoint" radius |
| `_ENTRANCE_PREFIX` | `waypoint_router.py` | `"nav_waypoint_entrance_"` | Prefix for auto-detecting entrance waypoints |
| `_START_CANDIDATES` | `waypoint_router.py` | 5 | Number of nearby ring waypoints evaluated for cost-based start selection |
| `_WALL_CHECK_RATIO` | `waypoint_router.py` | **6.0** (was 3.0) | NavMesh walk length ÷ XZ straight-line span between anchor and candidate. Above this, `_best_ring_index` skips the candidate as “likely wall”. **3.0 mis-fired** on legitimate long detours (~5.9× observed for `nav_waypoint_UA`), collapsing POI corridor vias; **6.0** matches production arena geometry better. Still a heuristic — tune here, not in the composer. |
| `_MAX_VALIDATION_QUERIES` | `waypoint_router.py` | 3 | Cap on NavMesh queries during ring-start candidate validation |
| `UPPER_SECTION_BRANCH` | `waypoint_router.py` | dict | Upper section → ordered branch prim names (staircase → … → section anchor) |
| `_DEFAULT_BRANCH_FLOOR_TOL_CM` | `waypoint_router.py` | 450 | `floor_hint_y` branch trim tolerance (cm) |
| `_JOIN_AT_SECTION_ANCHOR_MAX_CM` | `waypoint_router.py` | 700 | If `branch_join_pos` this close (3D) to section anchor, join at anchor only |

## "Use waypoints" toggle

- **Default**: enabled (checked)
- **Scope**: seat navigation has an explicit toggle. Restroom and quiet zone navigation always use waypoints (configured via `POI_CONFIGS["restroom"]["use_waypoints"]` and `POI_CONFIGS["quiet_zone"]["use_waypoints"]`). Exit routes, point-and-click, and spot navigation do not use waypoints.
- **When disabled**: `useWaypoints: false` is sent in the `seatNavigate` payload; backend skips `plan_route()` entirely and uses direct NavMesh shortest path
- **When enabled**: backend calls `plan_route()` with the seat's section letter

## Adding new waypoints

1. Add `def Xform "nav_waypoint_XX"` in `nav_waypoints_layer.usda` (inside the `over "World"` block)
2. Call `waypoint_router.invalidate()` if the ring was already cached (or restart the app)
3. For a new **entrance waypoint**: name it `nav_waypoint_entrance_NN` — auto-detected by prefix, no code changes needed
4. For a new **ring waypoint** (main corridor): just add the prim — it joins the ring automatically
5. For a new **upper-level section**: add the section waypoint prim, add a staircase access waypoint prim (if needed), and register the branch chain in `UPPER_SECTION_BRANCH` in `waypoint_router.py` (`_BRANCH_OFF_RING` is derived automatically)

## Adding waypoint routing to other navigation types

Seat and restroom navigation use corridor waypoints. To extend to other POI types, only `POI_CONFIGS` needs a change — no code modifications required:

1. Add `"use_waypoints": True` and `"route_id": "<poiType>_nav"` to the POI's entry in `POI_CONFIGS` (in `extension.py`).
2. That is all. `_waypoint_route_ids` is derived from `POI_CONFIGS` at startup, so `_on_calc` will automatically compute corridor waypoints for routes with that `route_id`. Distance estimation via `compute_routes_to_exits` also reads the `"use_waypoints"` flag from the config.
3. Optionally add a `useWaypoints` toggle in the corresponding frontend widget if user control is desired.

## Native NavMesh API usage

Distance-only queries (waypoint scoring, entrance detection, multi-segment measurement) use `navmesh_path_length_cm()` in `navmesh_shortest_path.py`. This performs `query_shortest_path` + `path_length_cm(path.get_points())` but skips the heavy `calculate_path_points` pipeline (position resolution, stage access, `Gf` conversion, endpoint snap validation is included). Much lighter than the full pipeline which is reserved for route drawing and path refinement.

`_is_segment_clear()` uses `path.length()` directly for its ratio check (straightening validation).

**API reference**: [Omniverse NavMesh API Commands](https://docs.omniverse.nvidia.com/extensions/latest/ext_navigation-mesh/api.html) — covers `INavigation`, `INavMesh`, `INavMeshPath` (`get_points`, `length`, `closest_distance`, `evaluate_position`, `evaluate_tangent`), area management, and `NavAgentDesc`.

